\section{How it works}{

A wrapper around GDAL's \code{gdal_translate -tr} (via
\code{\link[gdalraster:translate]{gdalraster::translate()}}) that writes a real Cloud-Optimized GeoTIFF
rather than a lazy VRT reference. Writing the file is the point:
\itemize{
\item The values get frozen once. GDAL's \code{average} resampling at a non-integer
scale factor gives \emph{different elevations depending on how the raster is
read} - reading the bundled tile at 2.5 m in one go versus in 137-pixel
windows disagrees by up to 0.53 m. Since every metric here walks its
input in tiles, a lazy reference would make results depend on
\code{tile_size}, which is the one thing this package guarantees never
happens.
\item Reads afterwards are about 10x faster than re-resampling on every read,
and you will normally run several metrics on the same coarsened DEM.
}

Overviews on the source are explicitly ignored (\verb{-ovr NONE}). GDAL would
otherwise quietly satisfy a coarsening request from whatever overviews the
file happens to carry, which silently overrides \code{method} - asking for
\code{"mode"} on a raster with averaged overviews returns averages, not classes.
}

\section{Choosing a method}{

\code{"cubic"} (the default) suits continuous surfaces - elevation, and any of
the continuous products here. It keeps the shape of the terrain better than
a plain block average, and it is \strong{NoData-safe}: a cell whose neighbourhood
is incomplete becomes NoData rather than being blended with the fill value.
The price is that holes grow a little - on the bundled tile, coarsening 1 m
to 4 m leaves 141 NoData cells against \code{"average"}'s 9.

\code{"mode"} (majority) is the one to use for \strong{categorical} rasters - the
class codes from \code{\link[=rvt_geomorphons]{rvt_geomorphons()}}. Each coarse cell takes the commonest
class beneath it, so the result still contains only whole class codes.
\code{"near"} also preserves codes but picks one arbitrary cell per block rather
than the commonest, so prefer \code{"mode"} unless you specifically want
subsampling.

\strong{Avoid \code{"average"} on anything this package wrote.} GDAL's average does
not exclude the NoData value from the mean, and every output here uses
-9999 - so cells beside a hole come out around -9000 instead of NoData.
It is safe only on rasters whose NoData is NaN (which the bundled DEM
happens to use). A warning is issued if you ask for it on a raster with a
numeric NoData value.

\code{"max"} is what the multi-resolution horizon search (\link{rvt_reach}) builds its
coarse levels with, because it is the only choice that keeps a distant
skyline intact: averaging flattens a narrow obstruction and nearest
neighbour can miss it altogether.

Any other algorithm GDAL accepts is passed through (\code{"bilinear"},
\code{"lanczos"}, \code{"min"}, \code{"med"}, \code{"rms"}, \code{"q1"}, \code{"q3"}, \code{"sum"}, ...) if you
know you want it. Note \code{"max"}, \code{"min"}, \code{"med"}, \code{"q1"}, \code{"q3"} and
\code{"sum"} are done with \code{gdalwarp} rather than \code{gdal_translate}, which cannot
perform them, and so need the raster to carry a coordinate reference
system.
}

\section{Which scale knob to reach for}{

Resolution is only half of scale - the other half is how far each metric
looks, and the two are not interchangeable. Coarsening removes the
microrelief \emph{before} the metric sees it; widening a search radius keeps the
microrelief and measures over more ground.
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=rvt_slope]{rvt_slope()}}, \code{\link[=rvt_aspect]{rvt_aspect()}}, \code{\link[=rvt_curvature]{rvt_curvature()}} and \code{\link[=rvt_log]{rvt_log()}} work off
a fixed 3x3 window and have no radius to widen - resampling is their only
scale control.
\item \code{\link[=rvt_slrm]{rvt_slrm()}}, \code{\link[=rvt_msrm]{rvt_msrm()}}, \code{\link[=rvt_dev]{rvt_dev()}} and \code{\link[=rvt_mstp]{rvt_mstp()}} cost the same per
pixel whatever their radius, so widening the radius is free and keeps more
information. Reach for that first.
\item \code{\link[=rvt_svf]{rvt_svf()}}, \code{\link[=rvt_openness]{rvt_openness()}}, \code{\link[=rvt_sky_illumination]{rvt_sky_illumination()}} and \code{\link[=rvt_shadow]{rvt_shadow()}}
cost roughly cells x radius, so coarsening pays twice over: fewer cells
\emph{and} shorter rays for the same reach in metres. Measured on a 4000x4000
0.25 m tile, sky-view factor over a 10 m reach takes 15.8 s; on the 1 m
version of the same ground, 0.6 s. That is often the difference between
practical and not on a large survey.
}

Every distance in this package is in \strong{map units}, so coarsening does not
silently change what a parameter means: \code{rvt_svf(dem, reach = 10)} looks
10 m out whatever the resolution. What changes is how finely that 10 m is
sampled - which is the point of coarsening.
}

